        <body>
            <div xml:id="d14">

                <pb facs="0142" n="24" xml:id="pg24"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Lawton to Mr.
                            Blaine.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1889-04-13"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 13, 1889</date>.
                        (Received April 27.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 128.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose copies of
                    further correspondence with the ministry of foreign affairs relative to the
                    banishment of Hugo Klamer from the Empire of Austria-Hungary, it will be
                    discovered that when I received the “additional information,” including action
                    on the appeal and the grant of leave to Klamer to remain here one month longer
                    than he had asked for, as shown by my first letter to the ministry, there was no
                    further interference with the liberty of the citizen of the United States which
                    presented any case for a demand at present. But it seemed to me that the
                    correspondence should not cease, even for a limited time, without a re-assertion
                    by this legation of the principle involved, and of the construction given by the
                    United States Government to the treaty of 1870. Perhaps the correspondence will
                    now be discontinued.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">A. R. Lawton.</hi></signed></closer>





